    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Start by adding 3 new Custom User Properties to the AppUser.cs class:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`using` `System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`namespace` `Identity.Models`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `class` `AppUser : IdentityUser`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `Country Country {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `int` `Age {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[Required]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `string` `Salary {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`}`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The Country property is an Enum, the Age property is of int type, and lastly the Salary property is of type string. We made the ‘Salary’ property as a required one by providing \\[Required\\] attribute to it."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Next, add a new class called Country.cs inside the Models folder which will serve as the enum for Country property. It’s code is given below."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `enum` `Country`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`USA, UK, France, Germany, Russia`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`}`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Now we have added **ASP.NET Core Identity Custom User Properties** to our app. Next we need to update the Identity Database. For this we will run the Entity Framework Core Migration commands."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "On the Package Manager Console window, run the following 2 commands:"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "data": {
        "title": "Code Example",
        "content": "Code example for How to add Custom User Properties in ASP.NET Core Identity",
        "language": "csharp",
        "code": "dotnet ef migrations add MigrationNew\ndotnet ef database update"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "When the commands complete, the SQL table called AspNetUsers of the Identity Database (that stores user records), will contain 3 new columns added to it. These are Age, Country and Salary."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Open the AspNetUsers SQL table and check these 3 newly added columns. This is shown by the below given image."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "data": {
        "src": "https://www.yogihosting.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/AspNetUsers-table.png \"AspNetUsers asp.net core identity custom user properties\"",
        "alt": "AspNetUsers asp.net core identity custom user properties",
        "caption": null
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Updating Create and Update User methods"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Now we update the Create and Update Action methods of Admin Controller, to add the support for the Custom Properties that we just added. First we add these 3 new properties (Country, Age & Salary) to the User.cs class."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`namespace` `Identity.Models`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `class` `User`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`{`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[Required]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `string` `Name {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[Required]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[RegularExpression(``\"^[a-zA-Z0-9_\\\\.-]+@([a-zA-Z0-9-]+\\\\.)+[a-zA-Z]{2,6}$\"``, ErrorMessage =` `\"E-mail is not valid\"``)]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `string` `Email {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[Required]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `string` `Password {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `Country Country {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `int` `Age {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`[Required]`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`public` `string` `Salary {` `get``;` `set``; }`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "`}`"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Next, we add 3 new HTML tags for these properties in the Create & Update Views so that users can enter the values for them during registration of a new user to Identity and also when updating a previous user. These views are located inside Views ➤ Admin folder. We use the input tags for the Age and Salary properties, and for the Country property we use the select tag."
      }
    },
